# NGCheck Frontend Development Rules

## Angular 15 Standards

### Component Structure
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./example.component.scss'],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush
})
export class ExampleComponent implements OnInit, OnDestroy {
  private destroy$ = new Subject<void>();
  
  constructor(
    private exampleService: ExampleService,
    private toastr: ToastrService,
    private logger: LoggerService
  ) {}
  
  ngOnInit(): void {
    this.loadData();
  }
  
  ngOnDestroy(): void {
    this.destroy$.next();
    this.destroy$.complete();
  }
  
  private loadData(): void {
    this.exampleService.getAll()
      .pipe(takeUntil(this.destroy$))
      .subscribe({
        next: (data) => this.handleData(data),
        error: (error) => this.handleError(error)
      });
  }
}
```

### Service Pattern
```typescript
@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class ExampleService {
  private apiUrl = `${environment.apiUrl}/api/example`;
  
  constructor(
    private http: HttpClient,
    private logger: LoggerService
  ) {}
  
  getAll(): Observable<Example[]> {
    return this.http.get<Example[]>(this.apiUrl).pipe(
      tap(data => this.logger.log('Fetched examples', data)),
      catchError(this.handleError<Example[]>('getAll', []))
    );
  }
  
  private handleError<T>(operation = 'operation', result?: T) {
    return (error: any): Observable<T> => {
      this.logger.error(`${operation} failed: ${error.message}`);
      return of(result as T);
    };
  }
}
```

### RxJS Best Practices
- Always unsubscribe using takeUntil pattern
- Use AsyncPipe in templates when possible
- Prefer declarative approach with observables
- Use appropriate operators (map, filter, switchMap, etc.)
- Handle errors in pipe with catchError
- Use shareReplay for caching observables

### State Management
- Use services with BehaviorSubject for simple state
- Implement facade pattern for complex features
- Keep component state minimal
- Use OnPush change detection where possible

### Form Handling
```typescript
// Reactive Forms
form = this.fb.group({
  name: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.minLength(3)]],
  email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]],
  phone: ['', [Validators.pattern(/^\d{10}$/)]]
});

// Custom Validators
static customValidator(control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null {
  // Validation logic
  return null;
}
```

### HTTP Interceptors
```typescript
@Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  intercept(req: HttpRequest<any>, next: HttpHandler): Observable<HttpEvent<any>> {
    const token = this.authService.getToken();
    if (token) {
      req = req.clone({
        setHeaders: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` }
      });
    }
    return next.handle(req);
  }
}
```

### Routing Configuration
```typescript
const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'feature',
    loadChildren: () => import('./feature/feature.module').then(m => m.FeatureModule),
    canActivate: [AuthGuard]
  }
];
```

### Module Organization
- Create feature modules for each major feature
- Use shared module for common components
- Implement lazy loading for feature modules
- Keep app.module lean
- Use barrel exports (index.ts)

### Component Communication
- Use @Input/@Output for parent-child
- Use services for sibling communication
- Implement smart/dumb component pattern
- Keep components focused and small

### Template Best Practices
```html
<!-- Use async pipe -->
<div *ngIf="data$ | async as data">
  {{ data.property }}
</div>

<!-- Track by for ngFor -->
<li *ngFor="let item of items; trackBy: trackByFn">
  {{ item.name }}
</li>

<!-- Conditional classes -->
<div [class.active]="isActive"
     [class.disabled]="!isEnabled">
</div>
```

### Styling Guidelines
- Use SCSS for styles
- Follow BEM naming convention
- Keep component styles scoped
- Use CSS variables for theming
- Implement responsive design with Bootstrap grid

### Performance Optimization
- Use OnPush change detection
- Implement virtual scrolling for large lists
- Lazy load images and modules
- Use track by functions in *ngFor
- Minimize bundle size with tree shaking
- Implement preloading strategies

### Error Handling
```typescript
// Global error handler
export class GlobalErrorHandler implements ErrorHandler {
  handleError(error: Error): void {
    console.error('Global error:', error);
    // Send to logging service
    // Show user-friendly message
  }
}
```

### Testing Standards
- Write unit tests for all services
- Test component logic, not implementation
- Use TestBed for component testing
- Mock dependencies appropriately
- Test error scenarios
- Aim for 80% coverage

### Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation
- Test with screen readers
- Maintain proper contrast ratios

### Integration with Backend
- Use environments for API URLs
- Handle loading and error states
- Implement proper error messages
- Use DTOs matching backend
- Handle pagination properly

### Third-party Libraries
- Use ng-bootstrap for UI components
- Implement toastr for notifications
- Use ngx-mask for input formatting
- Integrate SignalR for real-time updates
- Use moment-timezone for date handling

Remember: Keep components small and focused. Extract logic to services. Use RxJS effectively.